    "id": "train-1662",
    "question": "A tomato plant has 100 tomatoes. Jane picks 1/4 of that number for use in their house. After a week, she goes back and picks 20 more tomatoes, and the following week picks twice that number. What's the total number of fruits remaining on the tomato plant?",
    "gold": "15",
    "answer": "When she picks 1/4 of the number of tomatoes, she goes home with 1/4*100 = <<1/4*100=25>>25 tomatoes.\nThe total number of tomatoes remaining will be 100-25 = <<100-25=75>>75 tomatoes.\nAfter picking 20 more tomatoes the following week, the number of tomatoes remaining is 75-20 = <<75-20=55>>55 tomatoes.\nThe next week she picks twice the number she picked the previous week, that is 20*2 = <<20*2=40>>40 tomatoes.\nThe total number of tomatoes remaining after the final picking is 55-40 = <<55-40=15>>15 tomatoes.\n#### 15"
  },
  "generation": {
    "text": "To determine the total number of tomatoes remaining on the tomato plant, we will follow the steps outlined in the problem.\n\n1. **Initial number of tomatoes:**\n   The tomato plant has 100 tomatoes.\n\n2. **Tomatoes picked in the first week:**\n   Jane picks \\( \\frac{1}{4} \\) of the 100 tomatoes.\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{4} \\times 100 = 25\n   \\]\n   So, she picks 25 tomatoes in the first week.\n\n3. **Tomatoes remaining after the first week:**\n   Subtract the 25 tomatoes picked from the initial 100 tomatoes.\n   \\[\n   100 - 25 = 75\n   \\]\n   So, there are 75 tomatoes remaining after the first week.\n\n4. **Tomatoes picked in the second week:**\n   Jane picks 20 more tomatoes.\n   \\[\n   75 + 20 = 95\n   \\]\n   So, there are 95 tomatoes remaining after the second week.\n\n5. **Tomatoes picked in the third week:**\n   Jane picks twice the number of tomatoes she picked in the second week.\n   \\[\n   2 \\times 20 = 40\n   \\]\n   So, she picks 40 tomatoes in the third week.\n\n6. **Tomatoes remaining after the third week:**\n   Subtract the 40 tomatoes picked from the 95 tomatoes remaining.\n   \\[\n   95 - 40 = 55\n   \\]\n   So, there are 55 tomatoes remaining after the third week.\n\nTherefore, the total number of fruits remaining on the tomato plant is \\(\\boxed{55}\\).",
